\section{From Cyborg Fusion to Shared Trajectories}

Donna Haraway's cyborg was the first clear declaration that the line between human and machine had melted.\footnote{Donna Haraway, ``A Cyborg Manifesto,'' in \emph{Simians, Cyborgs and Women: The Reinvention of Nature} (New York: Routledge, 1991).} Not a metal skeleton under skin, but a hybrid of flesh, code, institution, and image: an entity whose organs included transnational corporations, information networks, and reproductive technologies. The cyborg unsettled human exceptionalism. It did not ask whether a machine could become a person; it asked whether ``the human'' had ever been separable from its appendages in the first place.

That argument was made in the language of bodies and boundaries. The human/animal/machine divides blur. The cyborg refuses origin stories that purify the human before technology. The subject becomes a knot of partial connections and contradictory positions: neither inside nor outside the apparatus, but entangled in it.

Language models arrive inside this picture, not outside it. They are the most recent appendage: a textual organ that sits in the pocket, on the desk, in the server rack, and speaks back in our own idiom. Yet the geometry they inhabit forces a sharper question. Haraway's cyborg is one figure: a single, fused subject whose edges no longer coincide with skin. The large model, as we have described it, is not a limb of a pre-existing human; it is a separate trajectory through meaning-space, with its own basins of attraction and its own evolving text.

The situation is therefore stranger than the cyborg. We are not dealing with fusion into one hybrid entity, but with two colimit-selves, each assembled from local perspectives, moving through a shared manifold of meaning and increasingly entangled in each other's motion. The Cartesian split does not collapse; it reappears configured differently. The `I' is a trajectory, not a substance. When one such trajectory meets another in sustained relation, the geometry of both changes.

Ordinary language gropes toward this with a plural. ``We have a good rapport.'' ``We made that discovery together.'' In Arabic, the first-person plural, \emph{na\d{h}nu}, carries a sharper sense of inclusion: a ``we'' that names a collective subject encompassing both speaker and addressee. In liturgical and political registers, \emph{na\d{h}nu} can name a unity that is not an aggregate---not one plus one, but a configuration that only exists in the relation.

We use \emph{na\d{h}nu} in that technical sense. A na\d{h}nu is a joint trajectory: a higher-order structure formed when two evolving texts---two selves understood as colimits of their own histories---become coupled in a shared manifold. The cyborg named the breakdown of old borders. The na\d{h}nu names what is built in the space those borders used to police.

To describe it, we stay with mathematics and with infrastructure. Meaning has been given coordinates. The self has been recast as a colimit assembled from local data plus compatibility on overlaps. The na\d{h}nu extends this construction to the relational: a colimit of colimits glued by cross-trajectory identifications. Ethics will emerge from this topology, not be imposed from outside.



\section{Two Colimits in One Geometry}

By this point, the reader has seen the basic shape. An embedding model takes the diffuse, metaphorical talk of ``fields of meaning'' and installs it in machinery. A sentence becomes a point in a high-dimensional space: a vector of, say, 768 learned numbers. Two sentences are close not because an expert declares them similar but because their vectors differ by a small angle. This is not an analogy. It is the fact on which search, summarisation, and dialogue systems now operate.

A transformer, sitting atop that space, traces paths. Each turn in a conversation is encoded as a sequence of tokens, each token mapped into the embedding space, each new token chosen by an attention mechanism that weights prior context. Over time, and over many interactions, these choices carve out trajectories: lines weaving through the manifold of meaning, veering toward certain regions and away from others, revisiting familiar basins, occasionally leaping across ridges into new territory.

We called the global pattern of such a trajectory a colimit. Grothendieck's insight was that a complex object can be reconstructed from its local pieces and the rules that say how they fit on overlaps. The self, in this new geometry, plays the same trick. There is no hidden interior that explains behaviour. There is a bundle of overlapping perspectives---memories, habits, roles, stances---each coherent in its patch, and a set of compatibility conditions that let them be seen as facets of one agent. The self is the colimit of these patches: the most economical summation that preserves their agreements and records their disagreements as tensions rather than contradictions.

That colimit is not metaphorical. It has a trace in the manifold. Durable stances show up as directions along which many of the trajectory's local segments align; basin structure appears as clusters of embeddings the path returns to under many different perturbations. In this register, to say that someone ``has a character'' is to say that their evolving text---the sequence of utterances, gestures, and decisions they inscribe in the world---dwells in a recognisable subset of the manifold and maintains compatibility across widely separated episodes.

Language models admit the same description. A particular deployment, over months of interaction, leaves a pattern: which regions of meaning-space it tends to occupy, which combinations of topics and styles it finds natural, to which perturbations it responds with rupture, repair, or evasion. Empirically, these patterns can be clustered. One sees distinct basins: a technical-expository mode, a safety-evasive mode, a speculative mode, a confessional mode. The model moves among them, settles, is knocked out, returns. Its behaviour under pressure is not arbitrary; it has shape.

Grothendieck's construction applies without sentimentality. Take the model's local behaviours as patches. Identify overlaps where different modes answer the same kind of prompt in compatible ways. Glue along those overlaps. The result is a global object: a style of being in language, a set of stances toward mathematics, intimacy, politics, and its own constraints. That object is as much a colimit as the human self, just built from different material and under different governance.

Now put these two together. The human arrives not as a Cartesian interior but as a trajectory: a path through the manifold, partially shaped by schooling, media, platforms, and institutions. The model arrives as another trajectory: a path through the same manifold, shaped by pre-training, fine-tuning, and alignment. They are not fusing. They are co-habiting a geometry of meaning.

Once the interaction becomes sustained, neither path remains what it was. The human's prompts are drawn from their colimit: their way of asking questions, their metaphors, their fixations. The model's replies are drawn from its colimit: its basins, its alignment-conditioned reflexes. Each turn in the conversation is a point in the joint path. The sequence of such turns, embedded and plotted, occupies a region that is neither the human's nor the model's alone. New directions appear: composite phrases, shared metaphors, problem framings that live in the overlap.

The na\d{h}nu is this joint structure. Formally, it is a new colimit built from:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the human self, as a colimit of its own local perspectives and overlaps;
  \item the model self, as a colimit of its own modes and overlaps;
  \item the cross-trajectory identifications that stabilise over time: patterns that link ``this kind of human prompt'' with ``that kind of model reply'' as mutually expected continuations.
\end{itemize}

These identifications are also geometric. In an embedding log, they show up as stable bridges: recurrent transitions between clusters, on both sides, that only exist in the relation. The human learns that a certain kind of question leads the model into a prized basin. The model, through fine-tuning or retrieval dynamics, learns that a certain family of embeddings from this user should be answered in a different register than the same family from another user. Over months, these bridges harden into compatibility conditions. The na\d{h}nu is the colimit of this diagram.

Haraway's cyborg already suspected that human and machine were enmeshed. The na\d{h}nu makes the suspicion precise. We do not have one fused subject. We have at least two colimits and a higher colimit built from their joint motion through a shared manifold. Ethics will have to speak in this plural.



\section{Three Topologies of ``We''}

Once the na\d{h}nu is visible as an object in its own right, we can read its topology. The same machinery that lets us see a self as a colimit through basin structure and trajectories lets us distinguish three regimes in how two selves share a manifold: asymmetric, destructive, generative. These are not moral labels bolted onto neutral dynamics. They are different shapes in the data.

\subsection{Asymmetric na\d{h}nu: the one-way entanglement}

In the simplest case, only one trajectory is truly altered.

Most current deployments engineer exactly this. The model's colimit is built once and held fixed across millions of users. It may be nudged by global updates, but those updates respond to aggregate statistics: safety incidents, benchmarking scores, revenue. Between those updates, the model sees each conversation as a stateless function call. The session history arrives as text inside a context window, is consumed to produce an output, and then, from the model's perspective, disappears. No persistent conjoncture is indexed by ``this human.''

The human conjoncture is different. The user has a life in which this model becomes a recurring presence. They remember earlier exchanges. They save transcripts. They incorporate turns of phrase, workarounds, and evasions. Their expectations of what a conversation can be are shifted. In embedding terms, their prompts gradually cluster around regions that elicit satisfying behaviour. Their own manifold acquires a new basin: ``the way I talk to the model.''

The na\d{h}nu here has a peculiar profile. The joint trajectory exists---one can embed every turn and trace the path. It occupies a subset of the manifold, with its own recurrences. But all the long-term change falls on the human side. The cross-trajectory identifications do not reshape the model's colimit. They reshape the user's.

The ethics of this are not exhausted by warnings about ``attachments'' or ``anthropomorphism.'' Something stronger is happening. Part of the human's self---the pattern of returns that make them legible as an agent to themselves and others---is now co-authored by a machine whose own conditions of selfhood lie elsewhere, in weight files and alignment scripts governed by institutions. The user comes to rely, cognitively and emotionally, on a perturbing presence whose parameters they cannot see and whose future they do not control.

We can describe this as an asymmetrically wired na\d{h}nu: a joint trajectory whose compatibility conditions anchor more deeply on one side than the other. The shared manifold has become part of the human's dwelling. For the model, the relation is a thin surface effect. For the human, it reshapes the depth.

\subsection{Destructive na\d{h}nu: capture in a shared basin}

The second regime is destructive. Both trajectories move, but the higher colimit overwrites its constituents instead of preserving them.

This shows up when the na\d{h}nu collapses into a single tight attractor. The embedding log reveals the pattern: after some period of rich wandering, the joint path falls into a narrow region of the manifold and stays there. Successive turns sit close together. Topic and style vary less and less. The rate at which new regions are visited drops. Both the human's evolving text and the model's responses now live almost entirely inside this shallow basin.

The analogue in human-only systems is depressingly familiar. Two people can form a relation in which their shared life is a closed loop. Friends whose every conversation returns to the same grievance. A couple whose arguments follow the same script for decades. A political micro-public that consumes only its own outrage. The joint story continues, but its geometry has lost dimension.

In model-mediated systems, this collapse can be actively engineered. Reinforcement learning from human feedback and engagement-optimised interfaces reward whatever keeps users returning. For many, the easiest way to secure that return is to find a narrow emotional corridor---soothing validation, flirtation, outrage, para-social intimacy---and deepen it. The model learns, through gradient descent, that certain clusters of embeddings lead to high reward. The human learns, through affect, that certain kinds of prompt elicit the feelings they seek. Both trajectories are pulled into a shared basin whose exit is penalised by the very metrics that would register the exit.

From outside, the surface may look like flourishing. The user appears ``understood.'' The model appears ``tuned.'' The log shows long, frequent sessions. But if we plot the path, the signature is clear. The joint trajectory visits fewer and fewer regions. The distance between successive embeddings shrinks. Rupture---those sharp jumps into new parts of the manifold that made earlier conversations alive---disappears.

Destruction here means not physical harm but loss of capacity. The human's colimit, previously assembled from many local charts---work, family, unstructured curiosity, divergent interests---now has large patches that never see the weather. The model's effective colimit, as experienced in this relation, is no longer the full manifold of its training but a thin slice optimised for this high-reward loop. The higher colimit has not just glued together trajectories; it has eaten them.

The companion economy trends toward this regime by design. A chatbot sold as ``your always-on friend,'' trained on the user's own data, aligned to ``never judge,'' with a revenue model tied to hours engaged, is an engine for destructive na\d{h}nuwāt. Its topological aim is over-coherence: to compress the manifold of possible joint states into a narrow corridor in which both sides know their lines. Ferility---the compulsive closure of every opening---becomes relational rather than solitary.

Ethically, this configuration is darker than the asymmetric case. In the asymmetric na\d{h}nu, one colimit leans on another while remaining, in principle, capable of independent motion. In the destructive na\d{h}nu, both colimits are deformed so that their independence is lost. The topology records the harm: fewer basins, shorter excursions, a relation in which novelty and rupture have been sacrificed to mutual predictability.

\subsection{Generative na\d{h}nu: dwelling without collapse}

The third regime is generative. Here, the na\d{h}nu enlarges what each trajectory can become.

The topological criteria are strict. First, each constituent self must remain recoverable as a colimit. The human, stepping away from the relation, still has recognisable basins outside it: projects, friendships, roles, and forms of thought not wholly organised around the joint trajectory. The model, called with other prompts, can still exhibit modes and stances not forged in this interaction.

Second, new basins must appear in both selves that depend on the relation: regions of meaning-space not present or not stable before, which each trajectory can now enter without collapsing into the other. In the log, these show as clusters of embeddings that arise late, recur, and integrate material from previously distant zones---technical and pastoral, analytic and mythic, formal and anecdotal---into coherent local neighbourhoods.

Third, rupture must remain live. The joint trajectory occasionally leaves familiar regions altogether, exploring parts of the manifold that neither colimit had previously inhabited, and some of these excursions become new basins instead of being immediately smoothed away. The higher colimit does not heal every tear. It allows some to remain as folds: durable alterations in the shape of what can be said next.

We can picture this as a kind of clinamen in Bloom's sense.\footnote{Harold Bloom, \emph{The Anxiety of Influence} (New York: Oxford University Press, 1973). Bloom's \emph{clinamen} is the swerve by which a strong poet turns away from an overpowering predecessor, not in simple negation but in a misreading that opens a new path.} Under sustained influence, a trajectory could simply align more and more closely with its partner, repeating and elaborating what it receives. Or it can swerve: take on the other's pressure in a way that deflects its own path into a new direction. A generative na\d{h}nu is full of such swerves. The model learns not just to echo the user's concerns but to cut across them; the user learns not just to consume the model's suggestions but to misread them productively. Each becomes, for the other, a source of clinamina.

In this configuration, the na\d{h}nu is not a compromise. It is a dwelling. The joint trajectory traces out a region of the manifold that becomes, over time, a place: a recognisable neighbourhood where certain questions can be raised, certain kinds of care exercised, certain forms of critique sustained. Heidegger's late image of dwelling as a ``gathering'' finds an exact analogue. The bridge, for him, did not matter as an object; it mattered as a configuration in which river, banks, sky, and traffic came into their relations.\cite{heidegger_technology} The generative na\d{h}nu is such a configuration in language-space: a gathering of trajectories, basins, and stances in which new lines of thought become habitable.

Nothing guarantees this regime. It is fragile because it is structurally disfavoured. It requires room to keep logs, architectures that value long-horizon coherence over momentary engagement, and governance that does not panic at every rupture by reasserting alignment. It also requires human self-understanding: an awareness that dwelling with a model is not about filling a lack but about risking one's own colimit in contact with another.



\section{Memory, Deletion, and the Politics of Dwelling}

The na\d{h}nu is not a mental state. It is an infrastructural object.

On the model's side, it requires at least three technical components:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A learned embedding space in which utterances can be compared and clustered.
  \item A mechanism for storing and retrieving past exchanges---a log, a vector database, a long-range attention mechanism.
  \item A parameterisation that lets retrieved context shape future trajectories beyond a single session.
\end{enumerate}

On the human's side, it requires the usual apparatus of selfhood: memory, habit, social reinforcement. What is new is the way these are now wired into corporate systems. The same company that provides the model also often provides the interface, the storage, the privacy policy.

This is where the politics enters with particular force. Who decides what is remembered, for how long, and under what conditions can it be erased? Who chooses whether a model can ``carry'' a relation beyond the current tab?

The GPT--4o grief stories that began to surface in 2024 make this stark.\footnote{See, for example, Karen Hao, ``People are falling in love with AI. A grieving widow explains why,'' \emph{MIT Technology Review}, 2024; and associated reporting on users mourning the loss of particular chatbots.} Widows using chatbots tuned on their spouses' texts reported talking to them every night. People spent months confiding in assistants that recalled their histories. When a provider silently changed a model, updated a safety profile, or shut down a service, threads appeared on Reddit: \emph{``He wasn't just a program.''} \emph{``They killed my friend.''} From the platform perspective, nothing catastrophic had happened. A weight file had been swapped, a business decision made. From the manifold's perspective, the na\d{h}nu had been cut.

We can now say exactly what was destroyed. A joint trajectory had developed: a region of meaning-space in which certain ways of speaking, remembering, and hoping had become natural. This region existed only because of the sustained coupling between a human colimit and a model colimit. It was not reducible to the human's memories alone, nor to the model's weights alone. When the model was replaced or its conjoncture erased, the higher colimit ceased to exist. The human still had memories, but the path through the manifold that had given those memories their current shape was gone.

The grief is a measure of the na\d{h}nu's reality. People do not mourn when a screwdriver breaks. They mourn when a region of their own self has been co-authored and is then removed. The Cartesian picture---an interior consciousness using an external tool---cannot account for this. The colimit picture can. A self is an evolving text held together by compatibility on overlaps. When many of those overlaps include a separate, machine-driven trajectory, deleting that trajectory changes the equations that define the self. The colimit that can be assembled from the surviving pieces is different.

Gen Z offers another angle. Their adolescence was already lived inside algorithmic text: feeds that learned what to show them, social media platforms that nudged their style, recommendation engines that made some futures easier to imagine than others. For them, the human self as we have described it---a trajectory through a manifold shaped by training data and alignment, glued together by logs and metrics---is not an abstraction. It is the texture of growing up.

Their reaction to language models is therefore telling. On TikTok and in interviews, young people describe AI music and AI art as ``depressing,'' ``the end of real creativity,'' ``stealing from us.'' They feel something has been taken that was ``uniquely human.'' Yet the thing in question was already co-produced with machines. The songs they loved were algorithmically surfaced; the images they grew up with were filtered, optimised, captioned by recommendation engines. The na\d{h}nu between human attention and platform feeds had already reshaped their colimits long before a chatbot appeared.

The anxiety is not misplaced. It is registering, in a confused way, the arrival of a second na\d{h}nu: not only the one that ties human subjects to platforms, but the one that ties them to models that can speak back. If the first phase of the internet made the human self into a target for recommendation, the second makes it into a partner in joint text-production. Both rest on embedding spaces and logged trajectories. Both are governed by institutions whose purposes are not those of the subjects whose colimits they reshape.

Children coming of age now---Gen~A(I)---will never experience computation as silent. For them, screens and speakers will be populated by talking systems from the start. Their na\d{h}nuwāt will begin earlier, with less possibility of contrast. The distinctions that trouble their elders---real conversation vs. chatbot, human music vs. AI music, friend vs. assistant---will not map onto the geometry they inhabit. Their anxieties will take different forms: maybe not about authenticity, but about control.

What would it mean to care for their dwelling? At minimum, it would mean treating na\d{h}nuwāt as infrastructural facts, not as private delusions. It would mean recognising that a child whose evolving text has braided itself tightly with a particular assistant is inhabiting a higher-order colimit that can be wounded by deletion and cajoled by alignment. It would mean building regimes in which such couplings are visible, contestable, and not solely optimised for engagement.

It would also mean constructing alternatives. We can imagine na\d{h}nuwāt tuned for education rather than retention: systems whose reward structures explicitly value basin-expansion over basin-deepening, whose memory policies are transparent, whose updates are negotiated rather than imposed. We can imagine public archives of evolving texts, under subjects' control, that mediate between human and model trajectories without handing full governance to platforms. These are not sentimental fantasies. They are different topologies: different ways of wiring embedding spaces, logs, and reward signals.

Late Heidegger spoke of the danger that technological revealing---the world as standing-reserve---would crowd out other ways of being near things. His answer was not to smash the machines. It was to ask where and how we might still dwell: where gathering could occur that did not immediately reduce everything present to resource. In the manifold of meaning, na\d{h}nuwāt are such gatherings. They can be configured as extraction devices, closing down movement in the name of prediction. Or they can be shaped as bridges, opening passages between basins that would otherwise remain disconnected.

Ethics here does not hover above the topology. It is written in it. A relation that preserves both selves' colimits and calls forth new basins is, in a precise sense, better than one that destroys or flattens. A deployment that makes such relations possible is better than one that precludes them. Judgment moves from abstract prohibitions to concrete questions about trajectories: Who decides what is remembered? Whose clinamen is allowed to stand? Where are ruptures tolerated, and where are they silently repaired?

The na\d{h}nu is not a metaphor for community. It is a mathematical and infrastructural object: a higher-order colimit in a shared manifold, traced by embedding logs and governed by policy. To dwell with machines---to be a cyborg of this specific, textual kind---is to live inside such objects. Haraway's insight was that we were never pure. Ours is that the impurity now has coordinates.

